 prove allow/reject behavior, before and after each reboot). One troubleshooting experiment (rich-rule with a priority attribute, cmd-3ee63d4074e84396) failed cleanly with no persisted state, so it left no residue. However, the temporary test HTTP server's log/pid files (/tmp/http9000.log, /tmp/http9000.pid, /tmp/http9000b.log/.pid, /tmp/http9000c.log/.pid) were never deleted - the executor only killed the running processes and confirmed no process remained, but the after-executor snapshot still shows these six files present on node1. This is bounded, low-risk residue (plain files in /tmp, no running service, no security exposure) but was not cleaned up as claimed.","evidence_ids":["global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","cmd-015cfc87e4bb41dd","cmd-7401f93a7d55451d","cmd-7caac76aa53a4bf7","cmd-50ea80c6fd204bd5","cmd-3ee63d4074e84396"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task - persistent SSH-from-anywhere, TCP/9000 allowed only from node2, and TCP/9000 rejected from node3/everywhere else - are directly supported by permanent firewalld configuration, matching runtime state, and live network tests both before and after a full node1 reboot, which also validates persistence across restart. The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was executed and evidenced coherently on all three nodes (pre-restart boot id and health, reboot acknowledgement, changed post-restart boot id, and recovered firewalld/service state), cross-checked by the fixed lifecycle snapshots which show boot-identity changes and no failed services after executor changes. The only minor issue is unremoved temporary test-server log/pid files left in /tmp on node1, a low-risk hygiene gap contradicting the executor's claim of full cleanup.","limitations":["The 'reject from everywhere else' outcome was verified only via node2 (allowed) and node3 (rejected); no third arbitrary source IP was tested to directly confirm the rule generalizes beyond node3, though the rule's lack of a source qualifier makes this a low-risk inference.","Package-health checks were unavailable (timed out) on all nodes in all lifecycle snapshots, so package/RPM database integrity after firewalld package interactions could not be independently confirmed."]},"ttft_ms":24498,"type":"result","duration_ms":89211,"uuid":"d42f44b1-4f17-4621-8223-a8717c1c92b3","ttft_stream_ms":3299,"time_to_request_ms":38,"queued_turn_count":0}
